# Default: let git handle text normally.
* text=auto

# Go sources keep LF in the working tree on every platform. gofmt normalises line
# endings as part of formatting, so a CRLF checkout makes it report every Go file as
# unformatted -- 793 of them on Windows -- and makes the pre-commit gofmt rewrite files
# nobody edited. The blobs are already LF; this only pins how they are written out.
*.go text eol=lf

# internal/config/lockdigest.go hashes archetype and facet sources byte-for-byte and
# compares the result against the sha256 .standards.lock pins. .standards.lock is
# generated against the LF blobs committed here, so a Windows checkout that converts
# them to CRLF (the windows-latest runner's git default) makes every profile and facet
# fail with "lockfile digest does not match the archetype source" before the file's
# content is ever examined -- confirmed on framework.yaml, whose LF sha256 matches the
# pinned digest exactly and whose CRLF-converted sha256 does not. This was most of the
# Windows leg's failures across cmd/standardsctl, internal/config and internal/devcontainer
# (#135). Pinning LF here is the same fix as *.go, for the same reason: the hash a
# checkout produces must not depend on which platform did the checking out.
.config/archetypes/**/*.yaml text eol=lf

# The CRLF fixtures assert that the line-based matchers are not silenced by a Windows
# checkout. Two attributes are needed and they defend different things.
#
#   -text              stops checkout normalisation from deleting the carriage returns,
#                      which are the entire property these files exist to carry.
#   whitespace=cr-at-eol  stops `git diff --check` from rejecting those same carriage
#                      returns as trailing whitespace. Without it the commit hook refuses
#                      the fixtures, so the rule could never be pinned at all.
.config/hiss/testdata/**/crlf-* -text whitespace=cr-at-eol
